WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.309
In 2001, Danish political

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.199
scientist Bjorn Lomborg burst

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.819
onto the international scene with

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.379
his bestselling and controversial

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.639
book, The Skeptical

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.899
Environmentalist.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.489
The one time member of Greenpeace

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.469
said that climate change is real and

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.089
that human activity is clearly

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.378
contributing to it.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.419
But he said the best science

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.189
didn't support the apocalyptic

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.169
visions put forth by people like

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.819
Earth in the Balance, author and

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.129
former Vice President Al Gore.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.479
Lomborg went on to create the

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.459
Copenhagen Consensus, a

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.619
think tank that applies cost benefit

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.479
analysis to problems facing

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.399
the global poor and works with

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.229
Nobel laureates, policymakers,

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.329
philanthropists and researchers

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.519
to develop pragmatic, relatively

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.409
low cost solutions to issues

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.689
such as tuberculosis, malaria,

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.549
lack of education and lack of

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:48.929
access to food.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.789
His new book is called Best

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.619
Things First, and it presents

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.659
what Lomborg says are the 12

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.489
most efficient solutions for

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.739
the world's poorest people.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.169
He argues that for about $35 billion

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.359
a year, a little more than half

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.098
of what the US spends annually on

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:06.959
humanitarian aid, these

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.789
policies would save 4.2

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.979
million lives and generate

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.799
an extra $1.1 trillion

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.259
in value every year.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.089
Reason caught up with Lomborg in

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.949
New York City during the latest

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.449
meeting of the United Nations

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.739
General Assembly.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.689
We talked about best things first

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.209
and why politicians and the media

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.859
continue to fixate on the

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.539
possibility of future climate

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.579
apocalypse rather than helping

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.469
the global poor in the here and

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:35.470
now.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.979
Bjorn Lomborg, thanks for talking to

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:44.609
us.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.109
It's great to be here. Thank you.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.189
Let's start off by talking about

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.709
your new book, Best Things First,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.419
which is based on the Copenhagen

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.739
Consensus project that you've

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.029
developed over the past couple of

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.199
decades. First.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.989
What is the Copenhagen Consensus?

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.489
So we're really a very small think

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.379
tank that work with hundreds

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.119
of the world's top economists to ask

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.039
very simple question How

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.629
do you get the biggest bang for your

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.459
buck? Look, there are lots

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.599
of stuff you can do.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.249
Not all of it is equally good.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.559
So we simply try to sort of push for

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.179
the really smart things you can do

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.469
for whatever it is that you want to

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.669
achieve. Do you want to get better

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.409
health care or do you want to do

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.819
something about global warming?

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.409
Or do you want to do good for the

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.359
world? The last the latter

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.979
thing is what this book is about.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.419
It basically asks a very simple

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.529
question If you're going to spend an

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.479
extra dollar or preferably a couple

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.649
hundred million dollars or

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.479
something, where can you get a lot

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.499
of bang for your buck?

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.689
Right. So the subtitle is The 12

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.909
Most Efficient Solutions

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.829
for the World's Poorest and

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.929
Our Global Sustainable Development

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.119
Goals Promises.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.039
First, what are the Sustainable

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.959
Development goals

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.179
that were hashed out in 2015

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.979
by the United Nations?

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.599
So they were essentially, you know,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.119
the UN's attempt to try to say,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.099
what should we do for the world?

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.509
What should we promise for the next

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.869
15 years from 2016 to

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:03.659
2030?

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.599
So in 2023, we're not halfway

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.579
sorry. We're at half time for these

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.859
promises, but nowhere near halfway.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.689
So we started out

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.899
saying, what should we promise?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.749
And obviously you want to, you know,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.579
reduce poverty or actually they

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.379
went so far as to say, get rid of

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.579
poverty.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.019
We should also reduce hunger,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.759
actually get rid of hunger, or we

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.839
should also fix war.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.219
We should fix corruption, we should

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.049
fix climate change, We should fix

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:28.959
education.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.079
But it ended up being

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.059
essentially an almost endless

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.219
list of promises to do all

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.049
good things in the world and make

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.849
sure that people get organic apples

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.059
and know about indigenous people

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.869
and have community gardens and the

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:43.859
whole shebang.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.449
Yeah, it's a very beautiful list of

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.769
all the stuff that you would ever

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:48.839
want if you if money

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.299
was no object.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.739
But it is.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.579
And U.N.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.009
Secretary General Antonio Guterres,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.839
he's calling for a $500

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.849
billion kind of global stimulus

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:00.929
project.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.819
You think that's generally wasteful

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.749
and unrealistic?

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.359
He's focusing mostly on

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.279
renewable energy, universal

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.919
social protection and job creation.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.839
Why is that misguided?

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.729
Well, so first of all, you're not

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.389
going to get people to give half a

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:18.148
trillion dollars more to a project

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:19.018
that's failing.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:20.639
So if you have a project where you

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.289
promised everything, then after

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.089
seven and a half years ago, wow, we

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.159
didn't make it give us more money.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.899
That doesn't really sound very

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.879
reassuring. But also, what you have

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.159
to realize is if you're actually

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.629
going to deliver on all of the

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.429
things you promised, half a trillion

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.779
dollars is not going to cut it.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.089
You will need somewhere between

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.919
10 and $15 trillion

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.329
to deliver all the things we

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.159
promised. And of course, that's

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.989
the same amount of money that all

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.819
the governments in the world

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.469
are right now raising in taxes.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.329
Essentially, the argument is,

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.769
if we want it to deliver on

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.109
everything we promised back in 2015,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.089
we'd have to double all global

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.090
taxes.

